WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.389
Last lesson, by the shop door, stood the apple dispenser.

2
00:00:06.389 --> 00:00:07.748
A lever, and no slot.

3
00:00:07.748 --> 00:00:13.185
Since apples only come out, an apple dispenser could stand in for a fruit dispenser.

4
00:00:13.185 --> 00:00:16.551
The arrow from apple to fruit carried straight over.

5
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:19.935
Now walk round to the back of the shop.

6
00:00:19.935 --> 00:00:21.327
There's a compost bin.

7
00:00:21.327 --> 00:00:26.454
Bruised fruit goes in, any kind at all: apples, lemons, whatever is past selling.

8
00:00:26.454 --> 00:00:30.315
Nothing ever comes back out, and nobody has ever asked it to.

9
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:35.736
One morning, a helper who is sorting apples asks you for a bin for bruised apples.

10
00:00:35.736 --> 00:00:37.576
You point at the compost bin.

11
00:00:37.576 --> 00:00:39.607
It's a perfectly good apple bin.

12
00:00:39.607 --> 00:00:42.526
It takes every fruit, so it takes every apple.

13
00:00:42.526 --> 00:00:46.396
The bin isn't fussy, and that's exactly what makes it useful.

14
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.076
But look which way that went.

15
00:00:49.076 --> 00:00:51.893
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

16
00:00:51.893 --> 00:00:53.485
Apple is the smaller type.

17
00:00:53.485 --> 00:00:58.875
So how can the bin for the bigger type be the one that fits, where apples are asked for?

18
00:00:58.875 --> 00:01:00.773
Did the arrow just turn around?

19
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:04.275
Let's write it down, in Kotlin first.

20
00:01:04.275 --> 00:01:07.594
A Bin has one function, drop, that puts an item in.

21
00:01:07.594 --> 00:01:12.671
CompostBin is a Bin&lt;Fruit&gt;: it takes any fruit, and prints it with its weight.

22
00:01:12.671 --> 00:01:14.818
And throwOut is the helper's job.

23
00:01:14.818 --> 00:01:19.244
It takes a list of apples and a Bin&lt;Apple&gt;, and drops each apple in.

24
00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:26.293
Hand throwOut the compost bin, with no extra word on Bin, and Kotlin says: argument type mismatch.

25
00:01:26.293 --> 00:01:30.124
Actual type is Bin&lt;Fruit&gt;, but Bin&lt;Apple&gt; was expected.

26
00:01:30.124 --> 00:01:35.209
Just like last time, a generic type is invariant until you say otherwise.

27
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:38.466
So let's run the substitution test.

28
00:01:38.466 --> 00:01:43.199
throwOut expects an apple bin, and we secretly hand it the compost bin.

29
00:01:43.199 --> 00:01:45.132
What can throwOut do with it?

30
00:01:45.132 --> 00:01:46.599
Can anything go wrong?

31
00:01:46.599 --> 00:01:47.665
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:53.666
All throwOut can ever do is drop an apple in.

33
00:01:53.666 --> 00:01:57.643
The compost bin takes every fruit, so it takes every apple.

34
00:01:57.643 --> 00:01:59.059
Nothing can go wrong.

35
00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:05.403
So we tell the compiler, once, on the declaration, with one small word: in.

36
00:02:05.403 --> 00:02:06.128
Bin&lt;in T&gt;.

37
00:02:06.128 --> 00:02:09.245
It's a promise that values of T only go in.

38
00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:10.940
Same call as before.

39
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.487
Nothing else changed.

40
00:02:12.487 --> 00:02:14.181
What does it print now?

41
00:02:14.181 --> 00:02:15.360
Pause and guess.

42
00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:23.434
It compiles, and BinDemo prints: compost, Gala, 180 grams.

43
00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:25.633
Compost, Fuji, 150 grams.

44
00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:34.076
The bin can even be stored as a Bin&lt;Apple&gt;, and one more Fuji, 140 grams, lands in the same bin.

45
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:35.887
Let's map it.

46
00:02:35.887 --> 00:02:40.049
The opening on top of the bin is a parameter: a T flowing in.

47
00:02:40.049 --> 00:02:43.938
The dispenser's lever was a return type: a T flowing out.

48
00:02:43.938 --> 00:02:48.168
And in is a sticker on the bin that says: slot only, no lever.

49
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.972
Now put the two side by side.

50
00:02:50.972 --> 00:02:52.780
Apple is a subtype of Fruit.

51
00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.685
With out, the dispenser keeps that direction.

52
00:02:55.685 --> 00:02:59.816
With in, the arrow flips: Bin&lt;Fruit&gt; is a subtype of Bin&lt;Apple&gt;.

53
00:02:59.816 --> 00:03:02.915
The bin for more stands in for the bin for less.

54
00:03:03.833 --> 00:03:06.395
So does it work the other way round, too?

55
00:03:06.395 --> 00:03:09.019
Can an apple bin stand in for a fruit bin?

56
00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:10.019
Pause and guess.

57
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:13.205
No.

58
00:03:13.205 --> 00:03:19.632
A Bin&lt;Apple&gt; might be the cook's pot for apple sauce, where every item is treated as an apple.

59
00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:24.281
Through a Bin&lt;Fruit&gt; view, anyone could drop a lemon into the sauce.

60
00:03:24.281 --> 00:03:27.220
Kotlin stops it: initializer type mismatch.

61
00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.887
Expected Bin&lt;Fruit&gt;, actual Bin&lt;Apple&gt;.

62
00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.816
A lemon in the apple sauce is the kind of mistake a kitchen only makes once.

63
00:03:34.816 --> 00:03:37.857
The compiler makes sure you never make it at all.

64
00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:40.568
I know what you're thinking.

65
00:03:40.568 --> 00:03:42.756
Fine, but I'd like to peek inside.

66
00:03:42.756 --> 00:03:46.680
Let me add a function, last, that returns the last item: a T.

67
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:48.086
Follow it through.

68
00:03:48.086 --> 00:03:53.224
Look at the compost bin through a Bin&lt;Apple&gt; view, and last promises an apple.

69
00:03:53.224 --> 00:03:55.530
But the bin may be holding a lemon.

70
00:03:55.530 --> 00:04:01.327
So the compiler refuses: type parameter T is declared as in, but occurs in out position.

71
00:04:02.233 --> 00:04:04.790
And that's where the bin picture breaks.

72
00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:09.904
A real compost bin gets tipped out every week, and anyone can see what's inside.

73
00:04:09.904 --> 00:04:12.652
A Bin&lt;in T&gt; gives you no typed way to look.

74
00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:15.145
Through in, values travel one way only.

75
00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:16.922
Now the name.

76
00:04:16.922 --> 00:04:18.436
This is contravariance.

77
00:04:18.436 --> 00:04:24.359
Contra means against: the arrow between the bins runs against the arrow between the fruit.

78
00:04:24.359 --> 00:04:28.176
And a type that only takes values in is called a consumer.

79
00:04:28.176 --> 00:04:30.611
So the compiler isn't being contrary.

80
00:04:30.611 --> 00:04:32.256
It's being contravariant.

81
00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:35.268
Java has no in on a declaration.

82
00:04:35.268 --> 00:04:43.281
Its Bin stays invariant, so the method says it where the type is used, with a wildcard, written question mark super Apple.

83
00:04:43.281 --> 00:04:47.681
Read it as: a bin for Apple, or for a type above it, such as Fruit.

84
00:04:47.681 --> 00:04:52.607
Leave it out, and javac says: Bin&lt;Fruit&gt; cannot be converted to Bin&lt;Apple&gt;.

85
00:04:53.533 --> 00:04:54.647
And the guardrail.

86
00:04:54.647 --> 00:04:56.998
Only make the in promise if it's true.

87
00:04:56.998 --> 00:04:59.906
T may appear in parameters, where values go in.

88
00:04:59.906 --> 00:05:03.680
Not in return types, and not in a val, where values come out.

89
00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.393
And the consumer you'll meet most often isn't one you write.

90
00:05:07.393 --> 00:05:12.219
It's a Comparator, which takes two values in, and only says which comes first.

91
00:05:13.133 --> 00:05:15.040
So, did the arrow turn around?

92
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.756
Yes, and for a good reason.

93
00:05:16.756 --> 00:05:21.014
Something that only takes things in doesn't mind how general it is.

94
00:05:21.014 --> 00:05:24.065
The bin for any fruit is happy with your apples.

95
00:05:24.065 --> 00:05:26.735
Say in, and the compiler lets it stand in.

96
00:05:26.967 --> 00:05:27.811
Quick check.

97
00:05:27.811 --> 00:05:29.921
A function needs a Bin&lt;Apple&gt;.

98
00:05:29.921 --> 00:05:33.719
You have a Bin&lt;Any&gt;, a bin that takes anything at all.

99
00:05:33.719 --> 00:05:34.844
Can you pass it?

100
00:05:38.033 --> 00:05:38.317
Yes.

101
00:05:38.317 --> 00:05:41.442
A bin that takes anything takes every apple.

102
00:05:41.442 --> 00:05:43.643
The arrow flips all the way up.
